   here would double-invert the lower triangle. #}

| base \ quote |{% for quote in grid %} {{ quote }} |{% endfor %}
|---|{% for quote in grid %}---:|{% endfor %}
{% for base, row in grid.items() -%}
| **{{ base }}** |{% for quote, cell in row.items() %} {{ '%+.2f' | format(cell.spread) if cell else '-' }} |{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

Ranked by conviction then by the width of the spread:

{# The tradeable cell prints every blocker, not only the ones that blocked. A
   tradeable pair carrying a marker reads "yes (event:unchecked)": the pair is
   tradeable as far as the engine got, and the parenthesised part says how far
   that was. Printing a bare "yes" there was issue #17, and it made an offline
   run's 28 unchecked rows indistinguishable from 28 cleared ones. Nothing here
   matches on the marker names, so a new one under ADR 0002 rule 4 renders
   without touching this template. #}
| Pair | Dir | Conviction | Spread | Base | Quote | Agreement | Tradeable |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
{% for bias in pairs -%}
| {{ bias.pair }} | {{ bias.direction.value }} | {{ bias.conviction.value }} | {{ '%+.2f' | format(bias.spread) }} | {{ '%+.2f' | format(bias.base_score) }} | {{ '%+.2f' | format(bias.quote_score) }} | {{ (bias.agreement * 100) | int }}% | {% if bias.tradeable %}yes{% if bias.blockers %} ({{ bias.blockers | join(', ') }}){% endif %}{% else %}{{ bias.blockers | join(', ') }}{% endif %} |
{% endfor %}

A marker in brackets beside `yes` is a check that did not run or one that ran
and could not finish, not a reason the pair was rejected. `event:unchecked` on
every row means the run had no calendar: the pairs are tradeable on
fundamentals and nobody has looked at the news. `event:unknown` means a
calendar was consulted and could not answer, which is the one that asks the
reader to look at a calendar themselves within the hour. This column runs them
together; the shortlist below labels them apart.

`Agreement` is the share of pillar *weight* pointing the same way as the spread,
not the share of pillars. One heavy pillar dissenting moves it further than one
light pillar dissenting, which is the distinction a headcount would lose.

